WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.960
Some consider Thomas Edison one&nbsp;
of the greatest inventors ever.

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:08.640
Edison was photographed next to&nbsp;
a lightbulb so often that people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:11.160
came to believe he invented it. But he didn’t.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:15.720
Several inventors had demonstrated&nbsp;
various versions of incandescent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:20.640
lights before Edison patented the first&nbsp;
commercially successful lightbulb.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.760
Edison never crafted something out of nothing.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:28.020
His genius lay in his ability&nbsp;
to craft something better.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:30.600
To perfect what was already available.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:36.780
His critics say his only real invention was&nbsp;
taking credit for the inventions of others.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:41.940
So, was he America’s greatest&nbsp;
inventor or…was Edison a thief?

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:43.380
You decide.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:47.280
Edison’s life changed when he was 15 years old.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:52.440
One day, when he saw a station official’s young&nbsp;
son playing by the tracks in the path of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:57.900
oncoming train, Edison rescued two-year-old&nbsp;
Jimmie Mackenzie before a train crushed him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:03.600
The boy’s father was deeply grateful and taught&nbsp;
Edison how to operate Morse code on a telegraph&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:09.660
machine. This was not easy to do; operators had&nbsp;
to quickly send out signals in dots and dashes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:13.200
which were converted into letters&nbsp;
that made up words and phrases.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:17.400
Edison began working full-time as&nbsp;
a telegraph operator in Michigan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:19.980
where he grew up, and later&nbsp;
traveled around America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:22.020
working at different offices for years.&nbsp;
(Started at telegraph office in Port Huron)

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.500
He was able to do this even&nbsp;
though he was moderately deaf.

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:29.400
He then decided to devote&nbsp;
himself to a life as an inventor.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:33.780
Well, actually, he mainly improved&nbsp;
upon what was already invented.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:40.440
In 1874, he developed an advanced version of a&nbsp;
telegraph machine that could send and receive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:45.300
four telegraph messages simultaneously&nbsp;
on a single wire - two in each direction.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:50.880
His quadruplex telegraph machine enabled&nbsp;
his former employer Western Union to save&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:55.500
money because the company could now send more&nbsp;
messages without having to build new lines.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:02:00.300
Western Union purchased the rights&nbsp;
to the invention for $10,000, around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:02.460
$260,000 in value today. 
&nbsp;

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:07.680
Edison used the money to build his great&nbsp;
laboratory at Menlo Park, New Jersey.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.740
He began by perfecting the telephone there.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:14.880
By 1876, Alexander Graham Bell had found a way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:19.200
transmit human voices over wires.&nbsp;
The telephone was revolutionary.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.220
Western Union commissioned Edison&nbsp;
to improve upon Bell’s invention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:27.060
to make it practical, and so Edison&nbsp;
worked on a telephone transmitter.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:31.920
While working to perfect the phone, he&nbsp;
also tried to record the human voice.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:38.520
The phonograph put Edison on the map - giving&nbsp;
him the nickname the “Wizard of Menlo Park”.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:43.680
He began to expand his factory…and&nbsp;
his appetite for other inventions.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:49.140
His big breakthrough came when he created&nbsp;
a successful incandescent lightbulb.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:52.920
As I mentioned earlier, others had&nbsp;
already been making light bulbs.

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:57.540
British inventor Joseph Swan obtained the&nbsp;
first patent for a light bulb in Britain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.540 --> 00:02:59.863
two years before Edison patented the lightbulb.

00:02:59.863 --> 00:02:59.940
https://patents.google.com/patent/US205144A/en&nbsp;
- electric lamp Sawyer and Man

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:03.120
Meanwhile, in America, William Sawyer and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:08.700
Albon Man had received a U.S. patent&nbsp;
for the incandescent lamp in 1878.

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:13.380
So what did Edison do? He improved&nbsp;
the bulb. Previous ones had only been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:15.360
able to work for merely a few minutes at a time.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:19.980
By October 1879, Edison had an “aha” moment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:25.440
His bulb with a carbonized filament&nbsp;
burned continuously for 14.5 hours.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.140
But that still wasn’t good enough for him.

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:33.180
He and his assistant tested thousands&nbsp;
of plant materials and finally came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:37.500
up with a filament made from bamboo&nbsp;
that could last up to 1,200 hours.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:44.580
In 1880, Edison received a historic patent&nbsp;
for an improvement to electric lamps.

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:49.320
It’s been debated whether Edison’s patent&nbsp;
infringed on Sawyer and Man’s previous patent.

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:55.020
Eventually, Edison’s American lighting company&nbsp;
merged with the Thomson-Houston Electric Company -&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.020 --> 00:04:00.540
which made incandescent bulbs under the Sawyer-Man&nbsp;
patent. Together, they formed General Electric.

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:06.240
On New Year’s Eve, 1879, Edison&nbsp;
lit up New York’s Christie Street,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:08.700
marking the dawn of the electric age.

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:12.960
This was at a time when the world&nbsp;
still relied on candlesticks.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:19.440
He then began to mass produce and sell them.&nbsp;
But he didn’t stop with just improving the bulb.

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:23.040
Edison then began working on building a system to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:25.800
carry electricity so people&nbsp;
could use his light bulbs.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:29.160
In 1882, he set up a large generator plant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:33.300
on Pearl Street in lower Manhattan&nbsp;
and lit the street with electricity.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:36.000
He became rich and famous.

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:38.040
And…ruthless.

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:42.000
Like when he tried to discredit&nbsp;
a rival form of electricity.

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:46.980
Edison’s generating system used&nbsp;
direct current, which has its flaws.

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:52.500
DC travels through a power line in one&nbsp;
direction at a constant voltage but can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:58.320
only go up to a mile or so before losing power, so&nbsp;
a generating plant was required every few blocks.

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:02.280
He hired a young engineer to&nbsp;
improve upon his DC generators.

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:07.860
But Nikola Tesla came up with a&nbsp;
better solution: alternating current.

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:12.960
AC periodically changes direction&nbsp;
and can also vary its voltage. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:15.420
can travel hundreds of miles without losing power.

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:19.500
Yet when Tesla tried to convince&nbsp;
Edison of the advantages of AC,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:21.840
his boss wasn’t having any of it.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:26.040
Edison didn’t want to lose the&nbsp;
royalties he earned from his DC patents.

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:29.580
Tesla’s AC system caught the attention of American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:33.360
industrialist George Westinghouse&nbsp;
who bought up Tesla’s patents.

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:38.940
AC generators were spreading fast across the&nbsp;
country - faster than Edison’s DC system.

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:45.720
So in order to discredit his rivals’ AC&nbsp;
system, Edison devised an ugly strategy.

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:51.329
He gave boys who lived around his New Jersey lab
25 cents for every stray dog they brought him

00:05:51.329 --> 00:05:55.018
and organized public electrocutions of&nbsp;
these dogs with AC.

00:05:55.018 --> 00:06:00.989
Edison took every opportunity to question&nbsp;
the safety of Tesla’s AC system, declaring:

00:06:00.989 --> 00:06:04.349
"Just as certain as death, Westinghouse will kill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.349 --> 00:06:08.549
a customer within six months after&nbsp;
he puts in a system of any size."

00:06:08.549 --> 00:06:12.149
Edison secretly funded the first electric chair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.149 --> 00:06:16.529
which used AC - despite publicly&nbsp;
denouncing capital punishment.

00:06:16.529 --> 00:06:21.509
The state of New York had commissioned his&nbsp;
former assistant Harold Brown to build the chair.

00:06:21.509 --> 00:06:26.669
Brown pushed to have it powered by AC&nbsp;
using a Westinghouse generator that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.669 --> 00:06:29.009
surreptitiously secured through another company.

00:06:29.009 --> 00:06:35.729
But his schemes didn’t work; AC would go on&nbsp;
to power the world, much to Edison’s dismay.

00:06:35.729 --> 00:06:40.589
Yet, Edison was more famous than&nbsp;
Tesla and certainly a lot wealthier.

00:06:40.589 --> 00:06:43.649
Tesla died very poor - I’ve done&nbsp;
a series of videos on what led&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.649 --> 00:06:47.069
to Tesla’s tragic demise which&nbsp;
I’ll link to in my description.

00:06:48.029 --> 00:06:53.069
Edison patented a whopping 1,093&nbsp;
inventions in his lifetime!

00:06:53.069 --> 00:06:58.949
He managed thousands of skilled scientists,&nbsp;
machinists, and designers who researched,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.949 --> 00:07:01.349
developed, and manufactured new technologies.

00:07:01.349 --> 00:07:05.549
So, where did Edison’s real genius lie?

00:07:05.549 --> 00:07:08.429
As Graham Moore writes in his novel The Last Days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.429 --> 00:07:12.089
of Night about the feud between&nbsp;
Edison and Westinghouse and Tesla:

00:07:12.089 --> 00:07:19.109
“His genius was not in inventing; rather, it&nbsp;
was in inventing a system of invention. Dozens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.109 --> 00:07:25.049
of researchers and engineers and developmental&nbsp;
tinkerers labored beneath Edison in a carefully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.049 --> 00:07:29.909
constructed hierarchical organization&nbsp;
that he founded and oversaw.” 

00:07:29.909 --> 00:07:35.069
In order words, Thomas Edison&nbsp;
was not a genius inventor or a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.069 --> 00:07:39.749
thief but a savvy businessman who&nbsp;
had a knack for managing people.

00:07:40.469 --> 00:07:45.809
It was his shrewdness as a manager that&nbsp;
allowed him to turn laboratory discoveries into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.809 --> 00:07:52.529
technological marvels in the marketplace even if&nbsp;
many of the original ideas came from someone else.

00:07:52.529 --> 00:07:55.409
Regardless of how you feel about Thomas Edison,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.409 --> 00:07:59.309
his contributions have&nbsp;
undoubtedly improved our lives.

00:07:59.849 --> 00:08:03.629
Whether you’re a fan of him or&nbsp;
Nikola Tesla - what they both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.629 --> 00:08:06.929
had in common was firm grasp of math and science.

